4,4,4,4,8,2,8,8,8,3,3,1,1],[8,1,6,3,1,2,8,8,1,1,3,8,2,3,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,3,8,3,8,3,3,1,3,1],[2,1,2,2,1,8,6,3,1,3,8,8,8,2,2,4,4,4,4,4,4,8,8,1,3,1,1,3,2,6],[1,1,3,6,6,3,2,2,8,2,6,3,8,6,2,4,4,4,4,4,4,3,1,1,3,3,8,3,3,3],[1,3,6,1,8,3,2,1,3,3,1,3,8,3,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,3,8,3,8,3,3,8,1,2],[8,8,3,8,8,2,2,2,2,8,8,6,8,3,3,4,4,4,4,4,4,2,2,1,6,1,8,6,8,6],[3,3,1,1,1,8,8,3,3,3,2,8,2,3,3,3,3,3,6,8,8,2,2,8,1,1,2,1,8,8],[6,6,3,3,3,6,3,3,8,8,8,8,1,3,6,8,3,3,8,1,1,1,6,1,2,6,2,6,3,8],[1,2,8,3,2,1,3,3,3,3,1,3,8,6,6,1,8,1,6,2,1,6,3,3,3,8,3,8,1,8],[8,6,2,6,3,8,2,8,8,3,1,1,6,1,1,2,1,8,1,2,1,3,1,8,3,6,3,3,3,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Each input is a 30×30 field of random-looking noise in a few colours. Hidden in the noise are a few large, perfectly solid rectangles, each a single colour. The fact sheet shows that every changed cell turns into colour 4, and all the changed cells form solid rectangular blocks:\n\n- **Pair 1:** a 5×9 block of 6s at rows 2–6, cols 5–13, and a 6×7 block of 8s at rows 16–21, cols 13–19.\n- **Pair 2:** four blocks:\n  - 1s, 6×7\n  - 2s, 6×7\n  - 8s, 6×6\n  - 3s, 5×5. Colour 3 is the background colour, so a rectangle can be in any colour, including the background.\n\n**Evidence from inspection.** I ran a greedy search that repeatedly takes the largest uniform rectangle not yet claimed. The areas split cleanly into two groups:\n\n| Grid | Real rectangles | Largest noise patch |\n|---|---|---|\n| Train 1 | 45, 42 | 6 or less |\n| Train 2 | 42, 42, 36, 25 | 5 or less |\n\nThe rectangles found in the train inputs are exactly the blocks recoloured in the outputs.\n\nOne detail matters: in pair 1, the connected 6-object has 50 cells, but only its 45-cell rectangular core becomes 4. The 6s attached outside the core stay. So the rule works on maximal solid rectangles, not on whole connected components.\n\nThe noise patches are always thin strips, at most 2 cells wide. The real rectangles are at least 5×5.\n\n**Rule.** Find every solid single-colour rectangle whose width and height are both at least 3 and whose area is at least 9. Paint each one with colour 4.\n\n**Test input.** The search finds three rectangles:\n- 8s, 7×10, at rows 4–10, cols 6–15. Here 8 is the background colour.\n- 3s, 7×6, at rows 19–25, cols 15–20.\n- 1s, 4×5, at rows 2–5, cols 21–25.\n\nThe next candidate is a 1×6 strip, which the width rule rejects. So these three blocks become 4.\n\n**Construction stages.** I start from the input and emit the canvas after each rectangle is painted, then emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    out = g.copy()\n    ok = np.ones(g.shape, bool)\n\n    def best_rect():\n        best = (0, None)\n        for c in np.unique(g):\n            m = (g == c) & ok\n            h = np.zeros(W, int)\n            for y in range(H):\n                h = np.where(m[y], h + 1, 0)\n                for x0 in range(W):\n                    mh = 10**9\n                    for x1 in range(x0, W):\n                        mh = min(mh, h[x1])\n                        if mh == 0:\n                            break\n                        wd = x1 - x0 + 1\n                        if mh >= 3 and wd >= 3:\n                            a = mh * wd\n                            if a > best[0]:\n                                best = (a, (y - mh + 1, x0, y, x1))\n        return best\n\n    while True:\n        a, r = best_rect()\n        if r is None or a < 9:\n            break\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = r\n        ok[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = False\n        out[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = 4\n        emit(out, f\"Found a solid {y1-y0+1}x{x1-x0+1} single-colour rectangle at rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1}; painted it 4.\")\n    emit(out, \"All large solid rectangles are recoloured to 4; the noise is unchanged.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Evidence from inspection.** I ran a greedy search that repeatedly takes the largest uniform rectangle not yet claimed."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"| Grid | Real rectangles | Largest noise patch |"}],"recolour":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The rectangles found in the train inputs are exactly the blocks recoloured in the outputs."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"All large solid rectangles are recoloured to 4; the noise is unchanged."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"11440e0d02335656f977f92e3bbdff7e830891a7a30b5cd2a5259038369e2aa3","task_sha256":"f9dc322c56aa28717619f60d1e7b3099598a6daeacc6841f62fca35015567189","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
